## Pages

| Route | Description |
|---|---|
| `/` | For You feed — all videos |
| `/following` | Videos from users you follow (login required) |
| `/explore` | Browse all videos with search |
| `/explore-users` | Find and follow users |
| `/upload` | Upload a new video (login required) |
| `/profile/[userId]` | Any user's profile page |
| `/live` | Live page placeholder |

---

## Key Features I Implemented

### Authentication
I built a full login and registration flow using a modal component. Authentication state is managed globally using React's Context API — once a user logs in, their info and JWT token are stored in localStorage and made available everywhere in the app through `useAuth()`. The Axios instance automatically attaches the token to every API request through a request interceptor, so I never have to manually add auth headers.

### Video Feed with Auto Play/Pause
The video feed uses the Intersection Observer API to detect when each video enters or leaves the viewport. When a video scrolls into view (more than 60% visible), it plays automatically. When it scrolls out, it pauses and resets to the beginning. This prevents multiple videos from playing at the same time and stops audio from overlapping — which was a real bug I had to fix.

Audio starts muted on page load because browsers require a user interaction before playing sound. After the first click anywhere on the page, all subsequent videos play with sound automatically — the same way the real TikTok works.

### Follow System with Persistent State
When the explore-users page loads, it fetches both the full user list and the current user's following list from the backend. This means the Follow/Following button state is accurate on every page load, not just after clicking. Clicking follow sends the request to the backend and updates the UI optimistically — the button changes immediately while the request is in flight.

### Video Upload
The upload page uses a `FormData` object to send the video file, thumbnail, title, and description to the backend as a multipart form. There's a video preview that shows the selected clip before uploading, and the title field auto-fills from the filename.

### Dynamic Profile Pages
Profile pages use Next.js dynamic routing with `[userId]`. Since Next.js 15 made `params` a Promise, I had to use `React.use()` to unwrap the params before accessing `userId` — something that wasn't in the original guide and took some debugging to figure out.

---

## Setup Instructions

### 1. Install dependencies

```bash
cd client
npm install
```

### 2. Create `.env.local`

```env
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:5000/api
```

If accessing from another device on the network, use your machine's IP:

```env
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://YOUR_IP:5000/api
```

### 3. Start the dev server

```bash
npm run dev
```

Frontend runs at `http://localhost:3000`

> Make sure the backend server is also running on port 5000

---

## Environment Variables

| Variable | Value | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` | `http://localhost:5000/api` | Backend API base URL |

---

## Dependencies

```bash
npm install axios jwt-decode react-hot-toast
```

These are installed on top of the base Next.js + Tailwind setup from `create-next-app`.